# Dialog script for a3ob_unpose, version 1

{
    name	a3ob_unpose
    script	a3ob_unpose
    label	"Unpose"

    help {
	"Moves your weighted mesh from the pose of the fitted skeleton back onto the Arma rest pose, by a cage from your body to the sample body."
    }

    inputlabel	1	"Your mesh"
    inputlabel	2	"Fitted skeleton"
    inputlabel	3	"Arma skeleton"

    parm {
	name	"method"
	label	"Method"
	type	string
	default	{ "cage" }
	menu {
	    "cage"	"Cage"
	    "bones"	"Bones"
	}
	help	"How the points move back to the Arma rest pose. Neither method changes the overall size."
    }
    parm {
	name	"bodygroup"
	label	"Body Group"
	type	string
	default	{ "body" }
	menutoggle {
	    [ "__import__('a3ob_houdini.entry', fromlist=['group_menu']).group_menu('unpose', 'bodygroup', kwargs)" ]
	    language python
	}
	help	"Cage method: the point group or primitive group of your mesh that is the bare body, the one Rig Fit wrapped onto. Its points always follow the cage. A piece outside it whose points follow one bone only (a helmet) is moved as a rigid piece; other points outside it (clothes that bend) keep their distance from the closest point of the wrapped body and move with that place of its surface. Empty means the whole mesh is body."
	parmtag	{ "script_action" "import hou\nimport soputils\nkwargs['geometrytype'] = [hou.geometryType.Points]\nkwargs['inputindex'] = 0\nsoputils.selectGroupParm(kwargs)" }
	parmtag	{ "script_action_help" "Select geometry from an available viewport.\nShift-click to turn on Select Groups." }
	parmtag	{ "script_action_icon" "BUTTONS_reselect" }
    }
    parm {
	name	"rigid_pieces"
	label	"Rigid Pieces Keep Their Shape"
	type	toggle
	default	{ "1" }
	help	"Move all the points of a rigid piece (made by Accessory Weights in Rigid mode, or an island outside the Body Group that follows one bone only) with one rigid transform, so the piece keeps its shape. A piece of one bone that stands beyond the cage (a helmet above the neck of the headless sample) is put at the joint of its bone: it keeps its offset to the joint, and the neck is carried along. Off: every point follows the cage (or its own weights in the Bones method), which can stretch a rigid piece."
    }
    parm {
	name	"keephead"
	label	"Head Keeps Its Tilt"
	type	toggle
	default	{ "1" }
	help	"Cage method: move the head (the points that follow the head bone, with the rigid pieces that follow it, such as a helmet) by a shift only, so it keeps the tilt it has in your mesh. The cage would turn it with the neck of the sample body, which is not your head's tilt (the sample body has no head to say what that is). The shift is the one the cage gives the base of the head; for a head beyond the cage (nothing of the sample there to follow) it is the move of the head joint, so the head keeps its offset to the joint, and the neck is carried along with it. Off: the head follows the cage."
    }
    parm {
	name	"keepfingers"
	label	"Fingers Keep Their Shape"
	type	toggle
	default	{ "1" }
	help	"Cage method: move each finger segment (the points that follow a finger bone) by one rigid transform, the one that takes the segment of the wrapped body to the segment of the sample body, and blend the segments by your mesh's bone weights (the palm follows the cage). The cage alone lets the lattice of the neighbouring fingers pull a finger (fingers of your mesh are packed, the sample's are coarse), which flattens and widens them. Off: the fingers follow the cage."
    }
    parm {
	name	"keepsoles"
	label	"Soles Keep Their Tilt"
	type	toggle
	default	{ "1" }
	help	"Cage method: turn each foot back about its forward axis by the sideways roll that the cage added, so the sole keeps the sideways tilt it has in your mesh (the cage rolled the feet onto their outer edges by 5 to 6 degrees, though the wrapped and the sample soles are both flat). Pitch and toe-out are not changed. Off: the feet follow the cage."
    }
    parm {
	name	"neckfade"
	label	"Neck Fade Length (cm)"
	type	float
	size	1
	default	{ "30.0" }
	range	{ 1! 100! }
	help	"Cage method: how far down the neck and chest the body is carried along with the head. The head is put at its joint, and the neck that leaves it moves with it. From the neck joint downwards the carrying fades out over this length, and the neck and chest are stretched there to meet the cage again. A longer fade spreads the stretch over more of the neck and chest; too long a fade carries the chest and the base of the shoulders and makes the stretch worse beside the neck. On the owner scene 20 to 30 cm worked best (the old fade was 24 cm). Shoulders, arms and the rest of the body do not follow the neck, whatever the length."
    }
    parm {
	name	"group"
	label	"Group"
	type	string
	default	{ "" }
	menutoggle {
	    [ "__import__('a3ob_houdini.entry', fromlist=['group_menu']).group_menu('unpose', 'group', kwargs)" ]
	    language python
	}
	help	"The points to unpose. Empty means all points. A point group or a primitive group (its primitives' points count) works. The menu lists your point and primitive groups of the input and shows the P3D name of a selection; picking adds or removes the group. The arrow button picks points in the viewport. You can also type group names, patterns or point numbers (for example 0-3)."
	parmtag	{ "script_action" "import hou\nimport soputils\nkwargs['geometrytype'] = [hou.geometryType.Points]\nkwargs['inputindex'] = 0\nsoputils.selectGroupParm(kwargs)" }
	parmtag	{ "script_action_help" "Select geometry from an available viewport.\nShift-click to turn on Select Groups." }
	parmtag	{ "script_action_icon" "BUTTONS_reselect" }
    }
}
